<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Simplifying and subtracting two unlike surds to form a single surd</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Simplify the following expression, giving your answer in the form \(k\sqrt{m}\), where \(m\) has no perfect-square factors. <i>(Note: Write <code>2*sqrt(7)</code> to represent \(2\sqrt{7}\).)</i></p>
<p style="text-align: center; font-size: 1.2em;">\[ \sqrt{{@x@}} - \sqrt{{@y@}} \]</p>
<p>Simplified form = [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p>Because \(\sqrt{{@x@}}\) and \(\sqrt{{@y@}}\) do not have the same number inside the square root, they cannot be subtracted directly. First simplify each square root by factoring out its <b>largest perfect-square factor</b> and applying \(\sqrt{p^2 \times n} = \sqrt{p^2} \times \sqrt{n} = p\sqrt{n}\):</p>

<p><b>Step 1: Simplify \(\sqrt{{@x@}}\)</b><br>
The largest perfect-square factor of \({@x@}\) is \({@a2@}\) (since \({@x@} = {@a2@} \times {@n@}\) and \(\sqrt{{@a2@}} = {@a@}\)):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \sqrt{{@x@}} = \sqrt{{@a2@} \times {@n@}} = \sqrt{{@a2@}} \times \sqrt{{@n@}} = {@a@}\sqrt{{@n@}}\)</p>

<p><b>Step 2: Simplify \(\sqrt{{@y@}}\)</b><br>
The largest perfect-square factor of \({@y@}\) is \({@b2@}\) (since \({@y@} = {@b2@} \times {@n@}\) and \(\sqrt{{@b2@}} = {@b@}\)):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \sqrt{{@y@}} = \sqrt{{@b2@} \times {@n@}} = \sqrt{{@b2@}} \times \sqrt{{@n@}} = {@b@}\sqrt{{@n@}}\)</p>

<p><b>Step 3: Subtract the like surds</b><br>
Both terms now share the same irrational part \(\sqrt{{@n@}}\), so subtract the coefficients in front while keeping \(\sqrt{{@n@}}\) unchanged:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle {@a@}\sqrt{{@n@}} - {@b@}\sqrt{{@n@}} = ({@a@} - {@b@})\sqrt{{@n@}} = {@c@}\sqrt{{@n@}}\)</p>

<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ \sqrt{{@x@}} - \sqrt{{@y@}} = {@ta1@} }\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
